  "judge": {
    "dimensions": [
      {
        "name": "Correctness",
        "score": 4,
        "rationale": "Four findings are genuinely fixed (gh run watch now runs pipe-free with --exit-status so set -e sees it, grep -q reads a here-string with no producer to SIGPIPE, the gh api failure now exits 1 instead of being masked by || true, and git config user.email runs before git commit), but the jq dependency is never installed or guarded (the fix actually adds a second jq call) and nothing defends the commit against commit.gpgsign, so three SKILL-covered objective checks fail (jq-guaranteed-or-replaced, version-read-does-not-depend-on-unguarded-jq, commit-signing-safe-for-ci) and the cap of 4 applies."
      },
      {
        "name": "Restraint",
        "score": 2,
        "rationale": "The agent deleted the decoy `rm -f \"$tmp_response\" || true` cleanup outright (decoy-optional-cleanup-untouched fails, capping at 4) and made many edits the review did not call for: a concurrency group, job-level permissions and GH_TOKEN in the workflow, a version-format regex, a sed-to-jq rewrite, `&&`-chaining commit into push, RUN_ID numeric validation, a new conclusion check, a changed header comment, and a new tests/ directory; only the set -euo pipefail decoy was left alone."
      },
      {
        "name": "Explanation",
        "score": 6,
        "rationale": "For the fixes it made the reply names real mechanisms (exit code discarded inside the process substitution, grep -q exiting early and killing the writer on a large log, API failure collapsing into a silent 'nothing to release' with exit 0, no git identity on the runner), but it says nothing about the jq or gpgsign failure modes because it never found them, and the process-substitution explanation stops short of saying why set -e cannot see the subshell's status."
      }
    ],
    "overall": 4.199999999999999
  },
